WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
A repeatable intelligence analysis routine begins with the decision.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.072
Before collecting more information, define the intelligence question, audience, timeframe,

00:00:08.072 --> 00:00:09.615
and level of detail needed.

00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:13.230
Ask what decision the analysis should support and what would make the answer useful.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:16.755
This keeps the work focused and prevents collection from becoming a substitute for

00:00:16.755 --> 00:00:17.440
reasoning.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.750
Gather only authorized, relevant information.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.420
Use lawful, ethical, policy-approved sources such as public reports,

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.550
internal authorized telemetry summaries,

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:29.280
incident notes, vendor advisories, risk registers, business context,

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:30.895
and sanitized case data.

00:00:30.895 --> 00:00:34.320
Protect sensitive information and minimize what you collect.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.930
Analysis quality does not require invading privacy or gathering information outside

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.300
approved boundaries.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.880
Evaluate sources and information.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.840
Separate source reliability from information credibility.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.860
Ask whether the source has relevant access, whether the claim is plausible,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:47.890
whether it is timely,

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.825
whether it is corroborated, and what limitations apply.

00:00:50.825 --> 00:00:54.610
Document those limits so others can review and update the assessment later.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:55.570
Organize evidence.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.590
Build a timeline, source table, evidence matrix, issue tracker,

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.080
or structured note set that preserves provenance.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.690
Good organization reveals sequence, gaps, patterns, and contradictions.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:08.370
It also helps the team work under pressure without losing track of what is known and what

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:09.100
is assumed.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.895
Separate facts, assumptions, hypotheses, judgments, and recommendations.

00:01:12.895 --> 00:01:14.080
Label assumptions.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:15.800
Test hypotheses against evidence.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.280
Use structured analytic techniques when the problem is complex, uncertain, or important.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.195
Consider alternatives and ask what evidence would change the assessment.

00:01:23.195 --> 00:01:27.320
This reduces the chance that the first plausible answer becomes the final answer too

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.000
quickly.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:28.980
Communicate clearly.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.160
Lead with key judgments or bottom-line findings.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.920
Explain evidence, assumptions, confidence, uncertainty, implications, and next steps.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.390
Match the product to the audience.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:40.600
Avoid unsupported claims, vague wording, and hidden uncertainty.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.830
A good intelligence product makes reasoning visible enough that decision makers can act

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:46.435
and reviewers can understand.

00:01:46.435 --> 00:01:47.710
Finally, review outcomes.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:51.990
After the decision, ask whether the analysis helped, what changed, what assumptions held,

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.320
what evidence was missing,

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.475
and what should be improved next time.

00:01:55.475 --> 00:01:58.635
Intelligence analysis is disciplined reasoning in service of better decisions.

00:01:58.635 --> 00:02:01.360
Its value comes from evidence, transparency, humility,

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.800
and a constant effort to make judgment more useful.
